\subsection{CAR--TSR Trade-off}\label{sec:tsr_collapse}

All fine-tuned models exhibit moderate TSR degradation relative to the Pi0.5 baseline: 74.7\% $\to$ 51.5--54.0\% at 30K steps, a reduction of ${\sim}$21--23 percentage points. The SDF loss is not responsible for this degradation. LoRA-only drops by 23.2 points (to 51.5\%), while SDF variants drop by 20.7--20.9 points (to 53.8--54.0\%). SDF-augmented models show TSR 2.3--2.5 percentage points above LoRA-only, within seed-to-seed noise. Table~\ref{tab:per_task_tsr} decomposes TSR by task. The degradation is non-uniform: Chocolate Pudding retains most of its pre-training capability (${\sim}$68--78\% across conditions), while Orange Juice degrades most (${\sim}$25--33\%). Milk and BBQ Sauce occupy intermediate positions (35--49\% and 59--69\%, respectively). No task collapses to near-zero; the per-task pattern is similar between SDF and LoRA-only conditions, confirming that the SDF loss term does not drive the degradation.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Per-task TSR (\%) at 30K training steps (seed means). All tasks retain meaningful success rates after fine-tuning. The degradation pattern is similar between LoRA-only and SDF-augmented training.}
\label{tab:per_task_tsr}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Task & Pi0.5 Base & LoRA-only & SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ & SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ \\
 & (3 seeds) & (2 seeds) & (3 seeds) & (3 seeds) \\
\midrule
Orange Juice      & 70.7 & 33.0 & 33.3 & 24.7 \\
Choco.\ Pudding   & 86.0 & 68.0 & 78.0 & 76.7 \\
Milk              & 66.0 & 46.0 & 35.3 & 48.7 \\
BBQ Sauce         & 76.0 & 59.0 & 69.3 & 65.3 \\
\midrule
Mean              & 74.7 & 51.5 & 54.0 & 53.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The TSR reduction reflects a distributional shift in the policy, not learned paralysis. All fine-tuned models retain substantial EE motion (mean displacement ${\sim}$0.39\,m, 0\% frozen episodes): the robot continues to move actively through the workspace. The simultaneous CAR improvement and TSR degradation arise from the same source. The demonstration data consists exclusively of collision-free trajectories; LoRA fine-tuning on this data teaches obstacle-avoiding motion patterns, raising CAR by ${\sim}$28 percentage points. The same distributional shift moves the policy away from the pre-trained manipulation prior, reducing task success by ${\sim}$21--23 percentage points. The non-uniform per-task pattern---Chocolate Pudding retains ${\sim}$68--78\% while Orange Juice drops to ${\sim}$25--33\%---suggests that vulnerability to fine-tuning varies by task, potentially reflecting differences in grasp geometry or coverage in the demonstration distribution.

